				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/bittermixin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3085453); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3085453&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606920951" alt="bittermixin" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3085453)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/bittermixin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3085453); return false;" >bittermixin</a></span> <span class="odate time_1507364104 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">07 Oct 2017 08:15</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3609018">
									

<blockquote>
<p>and that the &quot;gay&quot; element didn't ruin it</p>
</blockquote>
<p>We're a pretty tolerant lot. Most long-time members or even members of a few months will likely not care about who or what your SCP is- it's just a matter of how well the story is conveyed.</p>
<p>That said, this one went okay-ish with me (fairly mundane in all honesty- the body horror wasn't written in a sufficiently grotesque manner IMO, and the pacing felt a bit too quick. a lot more could have been done here.) up to the last log. It felt very dry and typical, and I didn't feel any strong characterisation from 3606-A. It's the 'strong and reserved' archetype that I've seen done before with far more success, so you lose a lot of punch there.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>this wasn't received as &quot;edgy&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Eeeh, not 'edgy', per say, but certainly melodramatic.<br />
I don't understand the significance of the final image either. Maybe we're not meant to know, but I feel as though there was some wasted potential there. It doesn't end on a particularly satisfying note.</p>
<p>Unique, certainly eerie, but ultimately doesn't carry its own weight. -1</p>
<p><strong>EDIT:</strong> I'll elaborate.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;hide</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>I don't think I was aiming for body horror with any of the details I implemented, or with how organisms rot.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Even if it's not what you were aiming for, I believed it had a strong presence from the start that stayed in the back of my mind for the remainder of the article- of course, that could just be me, so take that as you will.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Given your other comments, it seems like what you're looking for is some sort of additional anomalous effect</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Not really. I think a more rich character from 3606-A was what I needed from this article. Which leads nicely into my next point:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Keeping in mind, naturally, that the driving force behind this narrative is a personal encounter with some sort of unfathomable, but compelling, divine.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>dry and typical</p>
</blockquote>
<p>There's very little emotion coming through the words- '<em>They swallow. In him, I am swallowed. Within him, I see</em>' is clearly written in quite a dramatic manner, but feels wooden and stiff without anything supporting this type of character. Yes, the SCP article format doesn't naturally lend to much building of such a character, but I firmly felt as if the one interview wasn't quite enough. I would have liked to see an initial encounter, perhaps. The very early stages of contact with August. I don't like being immediately dunked into this strange mystical babble, and it does come across as quite obnoxious the more you read. It's not different. It's a very typical depiction of someone under a trance, spouting vague references and praise, and I think a subversion of that concept would have worked a little better. I think you've played it a little too safely.</p>
<p>'<em>He… He sat beside me. He was looking down the hill, over the river. I said his name… His… It came out wrong, I couldn't hold it in my mouth. And he… he smiled. At me!</em>' comes across in the same fashion- the stammering, the pronoun game, the faux mystique of it all. It's what your mind immediately goes to when you think of 'hypnotized' or 'in awe' or whatever you want to describe the state August is in as. I have to congratulate you on making a story have a pretty decent depth in such a short word count, but I don't think the brevity works to its advantage, and a lot more could have been done with regards to the character development.</p>
<p>Just as an aside:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I melted. I… And he… With me… We both…</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Should be:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I melted. I&#8230; and he&#8230; with me&#8230; we both&#8230;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Without the capitalization. This applies to any word following an elipsis.</p>
<p>I don't think you should change it, because the reception here is overwhelmingly positive and you've clearly struck a chord with the majority: I just wanted to push my silly $0.02 out there in the interest of your later articles. I hope to see something in future that I can get a little more involved in.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I didn't have a way to describe the final image without losing the ability for readers to perceive their own idea of what happened.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Then I'm unfortunately able to understand it. Maybe I'm missing something, but it appears meaningless from my perspective. Sorry. My -1 stands.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

